1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,720
It's zero degrees outside.

2
00:00:02,020 --> 00:00:06,749
Your heat pump is pulling heat out of that freezing air and pushing it into your house.

3
00:00:07,049 --> 00:00:08,428
That sounds impossible.

4
00:00:08,728 --> 00:00:09,549
It isn't.

5
00:00:09,849 --> 00:00:15,715
And understanding how it works explains why it can cut an electric heating bill by more than half.

6
00:00:16,015 --> 00:00:18,668
In this video, I'll walk through it step by step:

7
00:00:18,968 --> 00:00:27,594
why freezing air still holds heat, how a heat pump moves it, and what that means in dollars compared with electric baseboard heaters,

8
00:00:27,894 --> 00:00:31,933
using official efficiency ratings and real energy prices.

9
00:00:32,233 --> 00:00:34,542
Step one: cold air still has heat in it.

10
00:00:34,842 --> 00:00:37,008
Cold just means less heat, not none.

11
00:00:37,308 --> 00:00:47,565
To find true zero heat, you'd have to go all the way down to absolute zero, which is minus four hundred fifty-nine point six seven degrees Fahrenheit.

12
00:00:47,865 --> 00:00:56,159
Measured from that point, air at zero degrees still has about eighty-seven percent of the heat energy of air at seventy degrees.

13
00:00:56,459 --> 00:00:59,776
At thirty-two degrees, it's about ninety-three percent.

14
00:01:00,076 --> 00:01:04,473
At forty-seven degrees, a mild winter day, about ninety-six percent.

15
00:01:04,773 --> 00:01:08,389
Even at five degrees, it's still about eighty-eight percent.

16
00:01:08,689 --> 00:01:10,219
Think about that for a second.

17
00:01:10,519 --> 00:01:18,629
The difference between a mild day and a brutal one feels huge to you, but for the air itself, it's a small drop in total heat.

18
00:01:18,929 --> 00:01:21,471
So the outdoor air in winter isn't empty.

19
00:01:21,771 --> 00:01:22,785
It's full of heat.

20
00:01:23,085 --> 00:01:31,288
The problem is that it's at a lower temperature than your living room, and heat naturally flows from warm to cold, not the other way.

21
00:01:31,588 --> 00:01:35,148
A heat pump's whole job is to make that heat flow uphill.

22
00:01:35,448 --> 00:01:37,402
Step two: grab the heat outside.

23
00:01:37,702 --> 00:01:44,995
A heat pump circulates a special fluid called a refrigerant through two coils, one outdoors and one indoors.

24
00:01:45,295 --> 00:01:52,972
In the outdoor coil, the refrigerant is kept at very low pressure, which makes it even colder than the winter air around it.

25
00:01:53,272 --> 00:01:58,638
Because the refrigerant is colder than the air, heat flows from the air into the refrigerant.

26
00:01:58,938 --> 00:02:01,641
That's the normal direction: warm to cold.

27
00:02:01,941 --> 00:02:06,482
As it soaks up that heat, the refrigerant boils and turns into a gas.

28
00:02:06,782 --> 00:02:07,725
That's the trick.

29
00:02:08,025 --> 00:02:13,492
Boiling absorbs a lot of energy, so the gas now carries the heat it took from outside.

30
00:02:13,792 --> 00:02:17,235
It's the same thing that happens when water boils on your stove.

31
00:02:17,535 --> 00:02:21,032
The pot keeps taking in heat while the water turns to steam.

32
00:02:21,332 --> 00:02:27,721
Except the refrigerant boils at a temperature far below freezing, so the winter air is enough to do it.

33
00:02:28,021 --> 00:02:35,056
A fan on the outdoor unit keeps pushing fresh outdoor air across the coil, so there's always more heat to grab.

34
00:02:35,356 --> 00:02:36,592
Step three: squeeze it.

35
00:02:36,892 --> 00:02:41,714
The gas goes into a compressor, the part that actually uses electricity.

36
00:02:42,014 --> 00:02:45,908
Squeezing a gas into a smaller space raises its temperature.

37
00:02:46,208 --> 00:02:49,808
It's the same reason a bike pump gets warm when you use it.

38
00:02:50,108 --> 00:02:54,836
After the compressor, the refrigerant is now hotter than the air inside your home.

39
00:02:55,136 --> 00:03:00,063
And here's the key detail: the electricity the compressor uses isn't wasted.

40
00:03:00,363 --> 00:03:02,987
It also ends up as heat in the refrigerant.

41
00:03:03,287 --> 00:03:11,318
So everything that went in, the heat from outside plus the compressor's own energy, is now ready to be delivered indoors.

42
00:03:11,618 --> 00:03:13,856
Step four: release the heat indoors.

43
00:03:14,156 --> 00:03:17,043
The hot refrigerant flows through the indoor coil.

44
00:03:17,343 --> 00:03:25,309
Now it's warmer than your room, so heat flows out of it and into your house, again from warm to cold, the natural direction.

45
00:03:25,609 --> 00:03:28,018
A fan pushes that warm air through your home.

46
00:03:28,318 --> 00:03:32,481
And as the refrigerant gives up its heat, it turns back into a liquid.

47
00:03:32,781 --> 00:03:34,408
Step five: reset the loop.

48
00:03:34,708 --> 00:03:40,554
The liquid passes through an expansion valve, a small opening that drops its pressure suddenly.

49
00:03:40,854 --> 00:03:45,619
That makes it very cold again, ready to go back outside and soak up more heat.

50
00:03:45,919 --> 00:03:47,765
And the loop runs over and over.

51
00:03:48,065 --> 00:03:49,846
If this sounds familiar, it should.

52
00:03:50,146 --> 00:03:53,137
Your refrigerator does exactly the same thing.

53
00:03:53,437 --> 00:03:56,965
It pumps heat out of the box and releases it into your kitchen.

54
00:03:57,265 --> 00:04:00,608
That's why the coils on the back of an older fridge feel warm.

55
00:04:00,908 --> 00:04:06,295
A heat pump is basically a refrigerator that cools the outdoors and heats your house.

56
00:04:06,595 --> 00:04:09,396
Step six: why this beats an electric heater.

57
00:04:09,696 --> 00:04:13,813
An electric baseboard heater turns electricity directly into heat.

58
00:04:14,113 --> 00:04:17,601
One unit of electricity gives you one unit of heat.

59
00:04:17,901 --> 00:04:18,643
That's it.

60
00:04:18,943 --> 00:04:23,025
A heat pump uses its electricity to move heat, not to make it.

61
00:04:23,325 --> 00:04:26,993
So it can deliver more heat than the electricity it uses.

62
00:04:27,293 --> 00:04:38,466
Under federal rules, a new heat pump has to reach a heating rating that works out to about two point two units of heat for every unit of electricity, averaged over a season.

63
00:04:38,766 --> 00:04:46,273
In other words, it uses about forty-five percent of the electricity of a baseboard heater for the same warmth.

64
00:04:46,573 --> 00:04:52,534
In very cold weather, it works harder, because the gap between outdoor and indoor air is bigger.

65
00:04:52,834 --> 00:05:02,879
ENERGY STAR's cold-climate heat pumps have to deliver at least one point seven five units of heat per unit of electricity at five degrees Fahrenheit.

66
00:05:03,179 --> 00:05:08,651
Even then, that's about fifty-seven percent of the electricity of a baseboard heater.

67
00:05:08,951 --> 00:05:10,850
Where does that two point two come from?

68
00:05:11,150 --> 00:05:15,305
From the rating on every heat pump's label, called HSPF2.

69
00:05:15,605 --> 00:05:23,660
It measures how many B T U of heat the heat pump delivers for each watt-hour of electricity, over a whole heating season.

70
00:05:23,960 --> 00:05:30,153
One watt-hour of electricity turned straight into heat gives three point four one two B T U.

71
00:05:30,453 --> 00:05:32,293
That's what a baseboard heater does.

72
00:05:32,593 --> 00:05:37,922
The federal minimum for a new heat pump is an HSPF2 of seven point five.

73
00:05:38,222 --> 00:05:43,777
Divide seven point five by three point four one two, and you get about two point two.

74
00:05:44,077 --> 00:05:49,513
So when you shop, the higher that number, the less electricity it needs for the same heat.

75
00:05:49,813 --> 00:05:56,635
And if you live somewhere with hard winters, look for a model that meets ENERGY STAR's cold-climate criteria.

76
00:05:56,935 --> 00:05:58,425
Now let's put dollars on it.

77
00:05:58,725 --> 00:06:10,524
I started with how much energy typical homes in three different climates use for heating, from the government's household energy survey, and worked out how much heat actually reaches the rooms.

78
00:06:10,824 --> 00:06:14,817
Then I priced that heat with each state's average electricity price.

79
00:06:15,117 --> 00:06:23,120
In Florida, heating a typical home with electric baseboard heaters costs about seven hundred forty-seven dollars a year.

80
00:06:23,420 --> 00:06:26,054
With a heat pump, about three hundred forty.

81
00:06:26,354 --> 00:06:28,889
That's about four hundred seven dollars saved.

82
00:06:29,189 --> 00:06:37,801
In Georgia, about one thousand five hundred ninety-seven dollars with baseboard heat, and about seven hundred twenty-seven with a heat pump.

83
00:06:38,101 --> 00:06:40,198
About eight hundred seventy-one saved.

84
00:06:40,498 --> 00:06:48,179
And in Minnesota, where winters are long, baseboard heat costs about two thousand three hundred forty-two dollars a year.

85
00:06:48,479 --> 00:06:52,663
A heat pump at the federal minimum, about one thousand sixty-five.

86
00:06:52,963 --> 00:06:56,653
That's about one thousand two hundred seventy-six dollars saved.

87
00:06:56,953 --> 00:07:07,317
Even if we assume the heat pump only manages the cold-weather rating all winter long, it would cost about one thousand three hundred thirty-eight dollars in Minnesota.

88
00:07:07,617 --> 00:07:10,870
Still about a thousand dollars less than baseboard heat.

89
00:07:11,170 --> 00:07:17,753
The colder the climate, the more heat you need, and the more a heat pump saves over electric resistance.

90
00:07:18,053 --> 00:07:20,870
Notice that the percentage is the same everywhere.

91
00:07:21,170 --> 00:07:25,872
The heat pump always uses about forty-five percent of the electricity.

92
00:07:26,172 --> 00:07:29,254
What changes is the size of the bill it's cutting.

93
00:07:29,554 --> 00:07:32,533
So how many American homes already heat this way?

94
00:07:32,833 --> 00:07:43,329
According to the government's household energy survey, about seventeen point two million homes use a heat pump as their main heating, counting ductless mini-splits.

95
00:07:43,629 --> 00:07:52,863
And about ten point seven million homes still rely mainly on electric resistance heat, from built-in electric units to portable heaters.

96
00:07:53,163 --> 00:07:58,736
That's about eight point six percent of all homes, the group with the most to gain from switching.

97
00:07:59,036 --> 00:08:00,365
One honest caveat.

98
00:08:00,665 --> 00:08:03,125
Beating electric baseboard heat is easy.

99
00:08:03,425 --> 00:08:06,085
Beating a gas furnace is a different question.

100
00:08:06,385 --> 00:08:13,360
A gas furnace doesn't use electricity for heat at all, so the comparison depends on two prices instead of one.

101
00:08:13,660 --> 00:08:23,848
In Minnesota, where gas is cheap, a new gas furnace heats that same typical home for about five hundred forty-two dollars a year, less than the heat pump.

102
00:08:24,148 --> 00:08:27,174
In Florida, the heat pump wins against gas too.

103
00:08:27,474 --> 00:08:37,381
It all comes down to the price of gas versus electricity where you live, and I worked that out state by state in my heat pump versus gas furnace video.

104
00:08:37,681 --> 00:08:40,581
So that's how a heat pump pulls heat out of freezing air.

105
00:08:40,881 --> 00:08:43,097
Freezing air still holds plenty of heat.

106
00:08:43,397 --> 00:08:51,951
The refrigerant soaks it up outside, the compressor makes it hotter than your home, the indoor coil releases it, and the loop starts again.

107
00:08:52,251 --> 00:09:04,708
Because it moves heat instead of making it, it uses about forty-five percent of the electricity of baseboard heat at the federal minimum, and about fifty-seven percent even at five degrees.

108
00:09:05,008 --> 00:09:13,703
In a cold state like Minnesota, that's about one thousand two hundred seventy-six dollars a year compared with electric baseboard heaters.

109
00:09:14,003 --> 00:09:21,154
So if you heat with baseboards or space heaters today, the cold outside isn't a reason to rule out a heat pump.

110
00:09:21,454 --> 00:09:23,525
It's the reason the savings get bigger.

111
00:09:23,825 --> 00:09:28,735
The full heat pump versus gas furnace comparison is right here on the channel.
